1. Install a Free APK on Android Emulator

I downloaded and installed an free calculator app on an Android emulator using Android Studio Device Manager.

Result: The APK was installed successfully, and the calculator app opened properly on the emulator.


2. Test Screen Rotation

The installed app was opened and the emulator was rotated from portrait to landscape and then back to portrait.

Observations:
In portrait mode, all buttons and text were properly aligned.
In landscape mode, the layout adjusted to the wider screen.
After rotating back to portrait mode, the app returned to its normal layout.
No major UI issues or overlapping elements were observed.

Result: The app handled screen rotation properly.


3. Test the App Without Internet

The internet connection was turned off in the emulator. A feature that required an internet connection was then tested.

Observation:

The app could not load online data.
A message such as "No Internet Connection" or "Please check your network connection" was displayed.
After turning the internet back on, the online feature worked again.

Result: The app handled the no-internet situation properly by showing an error message.

4. Compare Emulator and Real Android Phone

The login or registration process was tested on both an Android emulator and a real Android phone.

Differences observed:

i. Speed: The real phone responded faster and felt smoother, while the emulator was sometimes slower.

ii. UI Experience: The UI looked similar on both devices, but screen size and performance differences affected the overall experience.

iii. Notifications: Notifications were received more reliably on the real device. On the emulator, notification behavior may depend on the emulator configuration and Google Play services.

Result: The main features worked on both devices, but the real phone provided a more realistic user experience.


5. Testing Push Notifications

For an app such as **Instagram or Zomato**, push notifications can be tested using both an emulator and a real Android device.

On the Emulator

Install and open the application.
Enable notification permission.
Trigger an event that should send a notification.
Check whether the notification appears in the notification panel.

On a Real Device

Install the application on the phone.
Allow notification permission.
Trigger an event, such as receiving a message or an order update.
Check whether the notification is received correctly.
Verify the notification title, message, icon, sound, and action when it is clicked.

Limitation of an Emulator

A major limitation is that **push notifications may not behave exactly like they do on a real device**. Emulator support can depend on its configuration, Google Play services, and network setup.

Conclusion: Push notification testing should be done on both an emulator and a real device, but the real device gives more accurate and reliable results.
